---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Short because the site genuinely does very little. The temptation with a page
 * like this is to copy a generic template full of clauses about marketing
 * preferences and third-party advertising partners that do not exist here.
 * A privacy policy describing processing that does not happen is worse than
 * none: it is the same failure as a citation pointing at the wrong paper.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const updated = "14 September 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site collects, which is almost nothing, and what the host logs on its behalf."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          This site sets no cookies and has no accounts, forms or comments. It runs one piece of
          third-party code: Cloudflare's privacy-focused analytics, which counts page views without
          identifying you. Everything else on the page is plain HTML with no scripts at all.
        </p>

        <h2>What we collect</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing you give us, because there is no way to give us anything: no form, no login, no
          comment box, no upload.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do count visits. The site uses
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a>, which
          adds a small script to each page recording that the page was viewed, roughly where in the
          world the request came from, what kind of browser and device it was, and which site
          linked you here if any. We look at that to see which pages are read and whether anything
          is broken. It is the only measurement on the site.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it does <em>not</em> do is the part worth stating plainly, because most analytics do
          the opposite. It sets no cookie. It stores nothing in your browser. It does not fingerprint
          your device, does not assign you an identifier, and cannot follow you between visits or
          to any other website. There is no profile of you to build, and no advertising network
          involved at any point. We see counts, not people.
        </p>
        <p>
          The data is held by Cloudflare and governed by their privacy terms rather than ours. We
          never sell it, share it, or combine it with anything else, because there is nothing else
          to combine it with.
        </p>

        <h2>What the host logs</h2>
        <p>
          The site is served by Cloudflare. Like every web host, Cloudflare processes the requests
          it serves in order to serve them and to protect the service from abuse. That normally
          includes your IP address, the page requested, the time, and the user agent your browser
          sends. This happens at the infrastructure layer, we do not add to it, and we do not
          build profiles from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloudflare also asks your browser to report <em>failed</em> requests back to it, which is
          how a host notices an outage it cannot see from its own side. Successful page views are
          not reported this way, and we never see those reports. That is hosting behaviour rather
          than anything this site measures, but it is a thing a page here asks your browser to
          send, so it is named rather than left out.
        </p>
        <p>
          What Cloudflare retains, and for how long, is governed by
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's own privacy policy</a>
          rather than by this one.
        </p>

        <h2>What your browser stores</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing that we put there. No cookies, no local storage, no fingerprinting: the analytics
          above are deliberately the kind that need none of those. Your browser will cache the page
          and its fonts the way it caches any site, which is a browser feature rather than
          something we set.
        </p>

        <h2>Fonts and other assets</h2>
        <p>
          Fonts are served from this site, not from a font network, so loading a page does not
          send a request to Google or anyone else. The site deliberately carries no images, no
          embedded video, no social widgets and no tag managers. Apart from the Cloudflare
          analytics script named above, every byte a page loads comes from this domain, and the
          site instructs your browser to refuse to run any script from anywhere else.
        </p>

        <h2>Links out</h2>
        <p>
          This site is an index, so nearly every page links to other people's. Following one of
          those links takes you somewhere with its own privacy practices, which are theirs and not
          ours. Links to sources are plain links with no tracking parameters attached.
        </p>

        <h2>Data about other people</h2>
        <p>
          The data published here describes countries, organisations, models, laws and
          aggregate populations. Individuals appear only where they are already named in a public
          record we are citing, such as the author of a paper or the publisher of a government
          document. We hold no personal data about readers and do not seek any.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Australian privacy law and the GDPR both give people rights over personal information
          held about them. The analytics described above do not identify anyone, so there is no
          record here that is about you and nothing for us to give you access to, correct or
          erase. If you believe that is wrong, or if something published here concerns you
          personally, <a href="/corrections/">tell us</a> and we will deal with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you would rather not be counted at all, any content blocker or the "do not track"
          setting in your browser will stop the analytics script loading, and the site works
          exactly the same without it. Nothing here is gated behind being measured.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this ever changes, it will change here first, and the date at the top will move. A
          change that affects what is collected about you, rather than merely how this page is
          worded, is also listed on the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a> with its date.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
